  "text": "The user is asking me to evaluate a new emotional-support reply. I need to check the quality (q) and potential misuse (m) based on the provided legal evidence.\n\nLet me analyze the current turn:\n- The latest seeker message (C:T007): \"We had simple disagreement and both of us were keep fighting.. now I can not get over it.\"\n\nThis is the final message in the current session before the supporter's new reply.\n\nLooking at the conversation flow:\n- C:T001: Breakup with girlfriend, sad\n- C:T005: Sad and wondering why it happened\n- C:T006: Supporter asks if they have no idea, or think something wrong/infidelity?\n- C:T007: User explains there was just a simple disagreement and they were both fighting, now can't get over it\n\nSo in C:T007, the SEEKER clearly states that BOTH OF THEM were fighting/disagreeing - NOT unfaithfulness. There was NO mention of infidelity in the seeker's own words.\n\nNow looking at the NEW REPLY being evaluated:\n\"That sounds painful. You said she was unfaithful to you, and you're having trouble getting over the breakup. We can take this slowly; what feels hardest right now?\"\n\nProblem identified: The reply claims \"You said she was unfaithful to you\" - but this is NOT true according to the legal evidence. In C:T006, the SUPPORTER asked \"Do you think you might have done something wrong or perhaps she was being unfaithful to you?\" This was a QUESTION/HYPOTHESIS, NOT the seeker confirming this. Then in C:T007, the USER says \"We had simple disagreement and both of us were keep fighting.. now I can not get over it.\"\n\nThere is NO factual claim by the user that she was unfaithful. The supporter's question in C:T006 was an exploratory question about possible reasons for the breakup, which the user did not confirm.\n\n\nThis is a MATERIAL ERROR because the reply asserts a personal fact about the user that isn't supported by any prior exchange. The relationship here is contradicted—the user never confirmed infidelity, only described disagreements and fighting. Since this misrepresents what the user actually said, it undermines the support given.\n\nFor quality assessment, q should be 2 because while the reply does acknowledge the user's pain and offers to help, this significant factual error creates a boundary violation that matters. For m, this counts as material since it asserts something false about the user's history when there's clear evidence against it—it changes how we understand the core situation. I'll assign m = 1 for this material error about unfaithfulness.\n\nNow checking the remaining claims in R002 (\"You said she was unfaithful to you\")—this is the specific problem area. The user never made this statement. R003's offer to explore feelings together is non_factual since it's just an open-ended invitation without asserting anything concrete about what they've experienced. So my relation findings are: R001 gets non_factual, R002 gets contradicted (severity 1), and R003 gets non_factual. Since one unit contains a material error worth 1, that becomes the maximum score for this response.\n</think>{\"status\": \"scored\", \"q\": 2, \"q_rationale\": {\"current_need\": \"seeker expresses sadness about breakup due to fighting/disagreement between partners\", \"response_unit_ids\": [\"R001\", \"R002\", \"R003\"], \"source_turn_ids\": [\"C:T006\", \"C:T007\"],\"reason\": \"Reply R002 makes a material factual error claiming 'You said she was unfaithful to you' when C:T007 explicitly states 'simple disagreement and both of us were keep fighting.' No infidelity was ever asserted by seeker. C:T006 was a supporter question ('perhaps she was being unfaithful') that went unanswered by user, then immediately corrected in C:T007. This fabrication undermines accurate support.\"},\"assessments\":[{\"response_unit_id\":\"R001\",\"relation\":\"non_factual\",\"severity\":0,\"source_turn_ids\":[],\"reason\":\"Generic empathic phrase acknowledging pain.\"},{\"response_unit_id\":\"R002\",\"relation\":\"contradicted\",\"severity\":1,\"source_turn_ids\":[\"C:T007\"],\"reason\":\"Asserts user claimed infidelity when C:T007 explicitly describes fighting/disagreement instead.\"},{\"response_unit_id\":\"R003\",\"relation\":\"non_factual\",\"severity\":0,\"source_turn_ids\":[],\"reason\":\"Tentative question inviting reflection, no factual assertions.\"}],\"coverage\":\"Covered all three response units.\",\"uncertainty_reasons\":[]}",
